{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-953e1fa93947795a", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\t2026 Asia Pacific Signal and Information Processing Association Annual Summit and Conference (APSIPA ASC)\n2\t\n3\tExploring Efficient Waveform Diffusion Models\n4\tfor Foley Sound Generation\n5\tRunwu Shi∗ , Chang Li† , Jiahui Li∗ , Jiang Wang∗ , Yaozhong Kang∗ , Nabeela Khan∗ , Linghan Fang‡ ,\n6\tBenjamin Yen∗ , Takeshi Ashizawa∗ , Kazuhiro Nakadai∗\n7\t∗ Institute of Science Tokyo, Tokyo, Japan\n8\t† University of Science and Technology of China, ‡ Technical University of Munich\n9\t\n10\tE-mail: shirunwu@ra.sc.e.titech.ac.jp\n11\t\n12\tarXiv:2607.29148v1 [eess.AS] 31 Jul 2026\n13\t\n14\tAbstract—Recent advances in diffusion models have enabled\n15\thigh-fidelity Foley sound generation directly in the waveform\n16\tspace. Existing waveform diffusion models primarily rely on timedomain architectures, such as CNN-based U-Nets and DiffWavestyle models, or frequency-domain Transformers modeling temporal dependencies. However, these systems are typically built\n17\twith large model capacities and substantial computational costs,\n18\tleaving compact and efficient waveform diffusion architectures\n19\tlargely underexplored. In this work, we introduce a DualPath (DP) architecture for waveform diffusion that performs\n20\tdimension-wise self-attention along both subband and frame\n21\taxes in the time–frequency domain. This DP design enables\n22\tfine-grained temporal–spectral modeling while maintaining high\n23\tefficiency. Based on the proposed DP backbone, we develop two\n24\tvariants: DP-DiT and DP-U-Net. Experiments on the DCASE\n25\tand FSD-Kaggle2018 datasets demonstrate their superior performance. Notably, the 3M parameter variant achieves performance\n26\tcomparable to models with more than 50M parameters. Audio\n27\tsamples are available at https://samplesdemo.github.io/DP-Foley/.\n28\t\n29\tI. I NTRODUCTION\n30\t\n31\tFoley sound generation aims to synthesize sound effects that\n32\tare tightly aligned with events and timing, requiring both high\n33\tperceptual fidelity and accurate fine-grained temporal structure.\n34\tA variety of generative paradigms have been explored for this\n35\ttask, including Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs) [1],\n36\tautoencoders [2], and diffusion models [3], [4]. In this work,\n37\twe focus on raw audio generation and adopt diffusion models\n38\tto generate high-quality Foley sound.\n39\tMany diffusion-based methods for audio generation have\n40\tbeen proposed, targeting diverse output spaces. These approaches can be broadly categorized into three classes: (1)\n41\twaveform-level generation, which directly synthesizes raw audio signals [5], (2) latent-space generation, which operates in a\n42\tcompressed representation space commonly learned by an autoencoder [6], and (3) acoustic-feature generation, which produces intermediate representations such as mel-spectrograms\n43\tthat are later decoded into waveforms through vocoders [7].\n44\tIn this paper, we adopt waveform-level generation, motivated\n45\tby both fidelity and efficiency considerations. Compared to\n46\tlatent-space or acoustic-feature approaches, waveform-level\n47\tgeneration enables end-to-end modeling without relying on\n48\tintermediate representations and separate decoders, thereby\n49\toffering a more compact formulation. Moreover, with ap-\n50\t\n51\tSound Class Label\n52\tTemporal Condition Signal (RMS)\n53\t\n54\tFig. 1.\n55\t\n56\tFoley Sound\n57\tGeneration\n58\tWaveform\n59\t\n60\tClass and temporal signal guided waveform generation.\n61\t\n62\tpropriate architectural design, waveform models can directly\n63\tcapture transient-rich and fine-grained temporal–spectral structures that are crucial for producing convincing Foley sounds.\n64\tAt the same time, it is worth noting that recent large-scale\n65\taudio and music generation systems often operate in compressed domains (e.g., neural audio codec tokens or learned\n66\tlatents) [8], largely because raw waveforms are extremely\n67\thigh-dimensional and sequences at audio sampling rates are\n68\tprohibitively long to model at scale. Rather than addressing\n69\tthe complexity through model scaling or domain compression,\n70\tin this work, we investigate the limits of direct waveform\n71\tdiffusion under strict parameters and compute budgets. In\n72\tthis context, we study lightweight architecture paradigms that\n73\tenable raw waveform synthesis with only a few million\n74\tparameters while maintaining competitive perceptual quality,\n75\tprioritizing efficiency, deployability, and end-to-end fidelity.\n76\tNevertheless, existing waveform-based diffusion backbones\n77\t(e.g., CNN U-Nets and DiffWave-style architectures) often rely\n78\ton very deep networks and relatively large parameter counts\n79\tto model high-dimensional raw signals. Meanwhile, fully selfattention backbones working in the time domain remain costly,\n80\tand recent alternatives that apply temporal attention in the\n81\ttime–frequency (TF) domain to predict complex spectrograms\n82\tto alleviate the long sequence burden [9], yet they typically\n83\temphasize global temporal modeling and may under-exploit\n84\tfine-grained Intra-frame and Intra-frequency structures that are\n85\tcrucial for expressive Foley synthesis.\n86\tMotivated by the fine-grained frame-path and subband-path\n87\tmodeling paradigm that has proven effective in speech separation and enhancement literature [10], we propose a Dual-Path\n88\t(DP) backbone that performs attention-based modeling along\n89\tboth the Intra-frame and Intra-subband axes in the TF domain.\n90\tBuilding upon this DP module, we instantiate two lightweight\n91\tdiffusion backbones: DP-DiT (DP Diffusion Transformer) and\n92\tDP-U-Net, both of which support class and temporal energy\n93\tsignal conditioning, as shown in Figure 1.\n94\t\n95\t\fWaveform\n96\t\n97\tWaveform\n98\t\n99\tTemporal Attention\n100\t\n101\txN\n102\t\n103\tCNN Block\n104\t\n105\tC\n106\t\n107\tCNN Block\n108\tResidual\n109\tCNN Block\n110\t\n111\tT\n112\t\n113\tWaveform\n114\t\n115\tWaveform\n116\t\n117\tSTFT + CNN 1D\n118\t\n119\tSTFT + CNN 2D\n120\t\n121\tB×C×F×T\n122\t\n123\txN\n124\t\n125\tDP Module\n126\tIntra-Subband Attention\n127\t\n128\tResidual\n129\tCNN Block\n130\t\n131\tTransformer\n132\tBlock\n133\t\n134\tLatent Block\n135\t\n136\tB×C×T\n137\t\n138\tTransformer\n139\tBlock\n140\t\n141\tResidual\n142\tCNN Block\n143\t\n144\txN\n145\t\n146\tCNN Block\n147\tUpSample\n148\t\n149\tT\n150\tWaveform\n151\tSTFT + CNN 2D\n152\t\n153\tCNN Block\n154\tDownSample\n155\t\n156\tIntra-Frame\n157\tPath Attention F\n158\t\n159\tF\n160\t\n161\tT\n162\t\n163\tDownSample\n164\t\n165\t...\n166\t\n167\t...\n168\t\n169\tC\n170\t\n171\tC\n172\tIntra-Subband\n173\tPath Attention\n174\t\n175\tTransformer\n176\tBlock\n177\t\n178\tCNN Block\n179\t\n180\tIntra-Subband Attention\n181\t\n182\t(B×F)×C×T\n183\t\n184\tIntra-Frame Attention\n185\t\n186\t(B×T)×C×F\n187\t\n188\txN\n189\t\n190\tTransformer\n191\tBlock\n192\t\n193\tResidual\n194\tCNN Block\n195\t\n196\tIntra-Frame Attention\n197\t\n198\tDP Module\n199\tDownSample\n200\t\n201\t...\n202\t\n203\tLatent Block\n204\t\n205\tDP Module\n206\t\n207\tB×C×T\n208\t\n209\tIntra-Subband Attention\n210\t\n211\tUpSample\n212\t\n213\tIntra-Frame Attention\n214\t\n215\tDP Module\n216\tIntra-Subband Attention\n217\tIntra-Frame Attention\n218\t\n219\tUpSample\n220\t\n221\t(a)\n222\t\n223\tWaveform\n224\t\n225\tCNN Block\n226\t\n227\txN\n228\t\n229\t(b)\n230\t\n231\tWaveform\n232\t\n233\tCNN 1D + iSTFT\n234\t\n235\t(c)\n236\t\n237\tWaveform\n238\t\n239\tCNN 2D + iSTFT\n240\tCNN 2D + iSTFT\n241\t\n242\t(d) Waveform\n243\t\n244\t(e)\n245\t\n246\txN\n247\t\n248\tWaveform\n249\t\n250\tFig. 2. Five model variants for raw waveform generation: (a) CNN U-Net, (b) DiffWave, (c) TF temporal-attention DiT (TF-DiT), (d) TF Dual Path-attention\n251\tDiT, (e) TF Dual Path-attention U-Net. Conditioning signals (class embedding, diffusion time step embedding, and RMS energy signal) are omitted for clarity.\n252\t\n253\tWe conduct experiments on DCASE and FSD-Kaggle2018\n254\tdatasets, and the results demonstrate that our DP-based models\n255\tconsistently outperform state-of-the-art baselines across all\n256\tobjective and subjective metrics. Notably, our architectures\n257\tachieve superior performance with significantly fewer parameters. This demonstrates that high-quality waveform diffusion\n258\tcan be realized at a remarkably small scale through optimized\n259\tdesign, providing a resource-efficient yet high-fidelity solution\n260\tfor deployable Foley generation.\n261\tII. R ELATED W ORKS\n262\tDiffusion models that directly operate on raw waveforms\n263\thave been widely explored for various audio tasks, e.g., audio\n264\tgeneration, speech enhancement, and source separation [11],\n265\t[12], [13]. To support direct waveform synthesis, several\n266\tdiffusion-based backbones have been developed in either\n267\tthe time or frequency domain, as summarized in Figure 2.\n268\tWe broadly group them into five categories: CNN U-Net,\n269\tDiffWave-style, TF temporal-attention DiT, TF DP-attention\n270\tDiT, and TF DP-attention U-Net. CNN U-Nets cover most\n271\tFoley waveform generators: T-Foley [5] adopts a CNN UNet and introduces Block-FiLM for temporal control using\n272\tthe RMS energy, while Mamba-Foley [13] augments a similar\n273\tbackbone with a Mamba unit at the bottleneck; both build upon\n274\tthe U-Net design popularized by DAG [14]. DiffWave [15]\n275\tfollows a WaveNet-inspired time-domain architecture [16],\n276\twhich has been widely applied for vocoder tasks [17]. In\n277\tthe frequency domain, Undiff [18] proposes FFC-AE, a convolutional network operating on complex-valued Short-time\n278\tFourier transform (STFT) spectrograms, and CQT-Diff, a CNN\n279\tU-Net that operates in the CQT domain [19]. There are\n280\talso transformer-based methods, some of which add the selfattention mechanism into CNN U-Net backbones, and [9] uses\n281\t\n282\ta transformer to predict complex STFT coefficients as temporal\n283\tsequences.\n284\tIII. M ETHODOLOGY\n285\tA. Overall Model Architecture\n286\tWe design two backbone paradigms, a DiT-style model and\n287\ta U-Net-style model, as illustrated in Figure 2(d,e). Both architectures share the same core module: DP Module equipped\n288\twith dimension-wise self-attention, including Intra-subband\n289\tand Intra-frame attention, as detailed in the next subsection.\n290\tAccordingly, we name our models DP-DiT and DP-U-Net. DPDiT processes the feature map at the original TF resolution\n291\twithout any downsampling. In contrast, DP-U-Net follows a\n292\tfive-stage encoder-decoder structure comprising two downsampling stages, a latent bottleneck stage, and two symmetric\n293\tupsampling stages. Both backbones share the same TF frontend and waveform generation head. The input waveform x\n294\tis transformed into a complex STFT spectrogram with size\n295\t2×F ×T , where F and T denote the number of frequency bins\n296\tand time frames, respectively. A 2D convolutional projection\n297\tthen maps this spectrogram to a C-channel feature map of size\n298\tC × F × T for subsequent processing. After being processed\n299\tby the DP-DiT or DP-U-Net, the features are projected back\n300\tand converted to the waveform domain via a transposed convolutional layer and inverse STFT, yielding the predicted noise\n301\tϵ. Specifically, DP-U-Net adopts 2D shuffle-based upsampling\n302\tand downsampling mechanisms. Utilizing PixelShuffle and\n303\tPixelUnshuffle layers combined with convolutions, DP-U-Net\n304\tscales the spatial resolution by a factor of 2 at each stage to\n305\tcapture multi-scale features.\n306\tB. DP block with dimension-wise self-attention\n307\tFigure 3 illustrates the architecture of the DP Module. This\n308\tmodule begins with an optimized Block-FiLM mechanism\n309\t\n310\t\fConv2D\n311\t\n312\tGEGLU\n313\t\n314\tLayerNorm\n315\t\n316\tReshape\n317\t\n318\tReshape\n319\t\n320\tSelf-Attention\n321\t\n322\tTemporal\n323\tCondition\n324\t\n325\tGEGLU\n326\t\n327\tMLP\n328\tLayerNorm\n329\t\n330\tB×C×F×T\n331\t\n332\tEt + Ec\n333\t\n334\tx2\n335\tTransformer Block\n336\t\n337\tRMS signal\n338\t\n339\tIntra-Subband & Intra-Frame\n340\t\n341\tFig. 3. Diagram of the DP module. The transformer block is applied twice\n342\t(Intra-subband and Intra-frame), shown once for clarity. Et and Ec mean\n343\tdiffusion step and class embedding.\n344\t\n345\tfor temporal conditioning [20]. Departing from the original\n346\tcascaded two-layer design, which employs two consecutive\n347\tFiLM-and-convolution blocks. We modify the structure into\n348\ta single-layer configuration per module. Despite the reduction\n349\tin depth, we align the operational resolution of Block-FiLM\n350\twith the intermediate TF representation. This enables finegrained, TF-bin-level control that provides superior temporal\n351\tcontrol accuracy. For class and diffusion step conditioning,\n352\teach module incorporates a diffusion timestep embedding Et\n353\tand a learnable sound class embedding Ec via the AdaLNZero mechanism [21]. This mechanism predicts scaling and\n354\tshifting parameters from the combined embedding Et +Ec for\n355\tmodulation. After this, given the feature map in RB×C×F ×T ,\n356\twhere B is the batch size, the module captures joint dependencies by alternating between Intra-subband and Intraframe attention. Specifically, the Intra-subband path reshapes\n357\tthe features into R(B×F )×C×T to model temporal dynamics,\n358\twhile the Intra-frame path reshapes them into R(B×T )×C×F to\n359\tcapture instantaneous spectral relationships. Each stage follows\n360\ta Pre-Norm architecture, integrating multi-head self-attention\n361\tand a feed-forward network containing GEGLU and Conv2D\n362\twithin a residual structure.\n363\tIV. E XPERIMENTS\n364\tA. Dataset\n365\tWe utilized two datasets to evaluate the proposed method.\n366\tFirst is the Detection and Classification of Acoustic Scenes\n367\tand Events (DCASE) task 7 dataset challenge [20]. This\n368\tdataset contains 7 classes of sounds, including dog bark,\n369\tfootstep, gunshot, keyboard, vehicle, rainfall, and coughing.\n370\tThe developing dataset contains 4,850 audio samples, and\n371\tthe evaluation dataset contains 700 audio samples. We use\n372\tthe official development and evaluation datasets for training\n373\tand evaluation. In addition, we utilize the FSD-Kaggle2018\n374\tdataset [22], which comprises around 11,000 clips across 41\n375\tsound event categories. We use the provided training set with\n376\t9,470 clips for training and randomly choose 10 samples per\n377\tclass for testing. All audio samples are sampled at 22,050 Hz\n378\twith a duration of 4 seconds.\n379\tB. Implementation Details\n380\tFor all DP-DiT and DP-U-Net variants, we compute the\n381\tSTFT using a window size of 510 and a hop length of 255.\n382\tThis yields a complex-valued TF representation of shape 352 ×\n383\t256 (time × frequency bins) for each 4-second audio clip. The\n384\t\n385\ttemporal conditional RMS signal used for temporal control is\n386\textracted from the input waveform with a frame length of 512\n387\tand a hop length of 128. For DP-DiT, the channel dimension is\n388\tset to C = 64. The DP module is repeated 32 times, resulting\n389\tin a total of 3.27M parameters. For DP-U-Net, we consider\n390\ttwo model variants that share the same channel dimension\n391\tC = 64 but differ in the number of block repetitions. The\n392\tstandard DP-U-Net adopts a five-stage encoder–decoder layout\n393\twith DP module repetitions of {1, 2, 2, 2, 1}, yielding 8.57M\n394\tparameters. We further introduce a lightweight variant, DP-UNet Small, which uses the same five-stage layout with a single\n395\tDP module at each stage, resulting in only 3.26M parameters.\n396\tWe train all of the models for 500 epochs using the AdamW\n397\toptimizer [23] with a learning rate of 1 × 10−4 . Denoising\n398\ttraining is conducted for 200 steps with a linear noise schedule\n399\tranging from 10−4 to 2 × 10−2 . For sampling, we adopt the\n400\tDDPM scheduler with classifier-free guidance (scale = 1.2),\n401\twhere 10% of the conditions are randomly dropped during\n402\ttraining for unconditional generation.\n403\tC. Compared Baselines\n404\tWe compare the proposed DP-DiT and DP-U-Net with several representative baselines, including T-Foley [5], MambaFoley [13], DiffWave [15], and a TF-domain DiT model. TFoley is a CNN-based U-Net with a recurrent unit in the\n405\tlatent stage for temporal modeling, for which we use the\n406\tofficial pretrained checkpoint and original sampling settings1 .\n407\tFor Mamba-Foley, we implement it using the official codebase\n408\tand train it from scratch under the same training settings as our\n409\tmethods2 . Both models are substantially larger than ours, with\n410\t74.09M and 58.81M parameters, respectively. For the sampling\n411\tsetting, we use their original guidance scale. We also include\n412\tDiffWave [15], augmented with Block-FiLM conditioning for\n413\ttemporal control, configured with 24 residual blocks and a\n414\tchannel dimension of 98, resulting in 12.58M parameters.\n415\tIn addition, we compare with a TF-domain DiT baseline\n416\tthat adopts the same Transformer block as the proposed\n417\tmethod, and predicts concatenated real and imaginary spectrogram components along the temporal dimension, as shown\n418\tin Figure 2 (c). The depth is set to 16, resulting in 21.39M\n419\tparameters. Directly applying DiT in the time domain would be\n420\tcomputationally expensive due to the high temporal resolution\n421\tof feature maps produced by the 1D convolutional head, which\n422\trequires specifically designed downsampling mechanisms that\n423\tare beyond the scope of this work, and thus a time-domain\n424\tDiT baseline is not considered. For all methods, we use 200\n425\tsampling steps and adopt the same DDPM sampling strategy.\n426\tD. Evaluation Metrics\n427\tThe evaluation includes both objective and subjective measures. For the objective evaluation, we use the Fréchet Audio\n428\tDistance (FAD) [24] to assess perceptual quality and the\n429\tEvent-L1 Distance (E-L1) to evaluate the temporal accuracy of\n430\t1 https://github.com/YoonjinXD/T-foley\n431\t2 https://github.com/FurioColombo/mamba-foley\n432\t\n433\t\fTABLE I\n434\tO BJECTIVE AND SUBJECTIVE EVALUATION RESULTS . T HE BEST RESULTS ARE SHOWN IN BOLD . D URING TESTING , THE RMS SIGNAL IS EXTRACTED FROM\n435\tTHE EVALUATION DATASET. R EAL DATA’ S FAD IS CALCULATED BETWEEN THE TRAINING AND EVALUATION DATASETS .\n436\t\n437\tDCASE (7 classes)\n438\t\n439\tFSD-Kaggle2018 (41 classes)\n440\t\n441\tSubjective evaluation\n442\t\n443\tMethod\n444\t\n445\tParams\n446\t\n447\tE-L1 ↓\n448\t\n449\tFAD-P ↓\n450\t\n451\tFAD-V ↓\n452\t\n453\tE-L1 ↓\n454\t\n455\tFAD-P ↓\n456\t\n457\tFAD-V ↓\n458\t\n459\tQuality ↑\n460\t\n461\tTime Align ↑\n462\t\n463\tReal Data\n464\t\n465\t–\n466\t\n467\t–\n468\t\n469\t18.82\n470\t\n471\t3.95\n472\t\n473\t–\n474\t\n475\t69.71\n476\t\n477\t8.41\n478\t\n479\t–\n480\t\n481\t–\n482\t\n483\tT-Foley [5]\n484\tMamba-Foley [13]\n485\tDiffwave [15]\n486\tTF-DiT\n487\t\n488\t74.09M\n489\t58.81M\n490\t12.58M\n491\t21.39M\n492\t\n493\t0.035\n494\t0.021\n495\t0.019\n496\t0.170\n497\t\n498\t34.97\n499\t29.65\n500\t52.00\n501\t116.76\n502\t\n503\t10.11\n504\t6.03\n505\t11.06\n506\t23.87\n507\t\n508\t0.058\n509\t0.028\n510\t0.019\n511\t0.110\n512\t\n513\t87.32\n514\t72.99\n515\t100.26\n516\t130.66\n517\t\n518\t17.14\n519\t12.22\n520\t15.83\n521\t32.83\n522\t\n523\t2.53\n524\t3.25\n525\t2.45\n526\t0.79\n527\t\n528\t3.04\n529\t3.66\n530\t2.84\n531\t0.82\n532\t\n533\tDP-DiT\n534\tDP-U-Net\n535\tDP-U-Net Small\n536\t\n537\t3.27M\n538\t8.57M\n539\t3.26M\n540\t\n541\t0.009\n542\t0.009\n543\t0.009\n544\t\n545\t30.07\n546\t27.48\n547\t27.98\n548\t\n549\t7.02\n550\t5.34\n551\t6.34\n552\t\n553\t0.007\n554\t0.006\n555\t0.007\n556\t\n557\t67.09\n558\t62.77\n559\t75.13\n560\t\n561\t13.53\n562\t11.55\n563\t11.98\n564\t\n565\t3.97\n566\t4.07\n567\t3.79\n568\t\n569\t4.46\n570\t4.47\n571\t4.30\n572\t\n573\tTABLE II\n574\tI NFERENCE EFFICIENCY AND MODEL COMPLEXITY.\n575\tMethod\n576\t\n577\tRTF ↓\n578\t\n579\tGFLOPs ↓\n580\t\n581\tMemory (GB)\n582\t\n583\tT-Foley [5]\n584\tMamba-Foley [13]\n585\tDiffWave [15]\n586\tTF-DiT\n587\t\n588\t2.61\n589\t4.05\n590\t6.24\n591\t6.85\n592\t\n593\t54.15\n594\t64.67\n595\t1302.84\n596\t9.58\n597\t\n598\t2.11\n599\t0.44\n600\t9.74\n601\t0.12\n602\t\n603\tDP-DiT\n604\tDP-U-Net\n605\tDP-U-Net Small\n606\t\n607\t31.06\n608\t4.37\n609\t3.20\n610\t\n611\t349.53\n612\t196.78\n613\t78.38\n614\t\n615\t1.12\n616\t1.22\n617\t1.15\n618\t\n619\t140\n620\t\n621\tA. Objective and Subjective Evaluation\n622\tThe left panel of Table I presents the objective results for\n623\tDCASE and FSD-Kaggle2018. Overall, the proposed DP-DiT\n624\tand DP-U-Net variants achieve strong performance across all\n625\tmetrics with substantially fewer parameters than baselines.\n626\tSpecifically, DP-U-Net obtains the lowest E-L1 and FAD\n627\tscores, indicating superior temporal alignment and perceptual\n628\tquality, while DP-U-Net Small (3.26M) remains highly competitive. The performance gap between DP-U-Net and DP-DiT\n629\tfurther underscores the architectural advantage of U-Net over\n630\tDiT for waveform generation. Among the baselines, MambaFoley outperforms T-Foley, suggesting the importance of latent\n631\tspace design. Diffwave achieves relatively low E-L1 but suffers\n632\tfrom high FAD scores, indicating limited perceptual fidelity.\n633\tConversely, TF-DiT performs poorly, confirming that direct\n634\tspectrogram modeling along the temporal axis is inadequate.\n635\tIn summary, our models benefit from more effective inductive\n636\tbiases, enabling substantial parameter reduction while maintaining strong temporal accuracy and perceptual quality.\n637\tSubjective evaluation results are summarized in the right\n638\tpanel of Table I. Our proposed DP-U-Net consistently achieves\n639\tthe highest scores in both Audio Quality and Temporal Align-\n640\t\n641\tw/o Intra-frame\n642\tw/o Intra-subband\n643\t\n644\t60\n645\t50\n646\t\n647\tFAD-V\n648\t\n649\t120\n650\t100\n651\t\n652\t40\n653\t30\n654\t20\n655\t\n656\t80\n657\t\n658\tV. R ESULTS\n659\t\n660\t70\n661\t\n662\tw/o Intra-frame\n663\tw/o Intra-subband\n664\t\n665\t160\n666\t\n667\tFAD-P\n668\t\n669\tgenerated sound, following previous works [5], [13]. The FAD\n670\tis computed using VGGish (FAD-V) and PANNs-CNN14-32k\n671\t(FAD-P) embeddings implemented in the fadtk toolkit. The\n672\tE-L1 distance is computed on RMS energy feature\n673\tPNof audio\n674\twaveform, and is defined as E-L1(E, Ê) = N1 i=1 ∥Ei −\n675\tÊi ∥1 , where Ei and Êi denote the RMS energy features of\n676\tthe i-th frame from the reference and generated audio [20].\n677\tFor subjective evaluation, we conduct a 20-minute listening\n678\ttest with nine participants using a 5-point Likert scale. Two\n679\tkey dimensions are evaluated: Audio Quality, which measures\n680\tperceptual naturalness, and Time Alignment, which measures\n681\tsynchronization with the conditioning signal. All test samples are provided on the demo page. For each sample, we\n682\tpresent the reference audio together with the waveform and\n683\tRMS energy curves, allowing participants to assess temporal\n684\tsynchronization through both listening and visual inspection.\n685\t\n686\t1\n687\t\n688\tBaseline (Full): 75.13\n689\t2\n690\t\n691\t3\n692\t\n693\tU-Net stage\n694\t\n695\t4\n696\t\n697\t10\n698\t5\n699\t\n700\t1\n701\t\n702\tBaseline (Full): 11.98\n703\t2\n704\t\n705\t3\n706\t\n707\tU-Net stage\n708\t\n709\t4\n710\t\n711\t5\n712\t\n713\tFig. 4. Layer-wise ablation on the 5-stage DP-U-Net Small by independently\n714\tomitting a single attention block at each stage.\n715\t\n716\tment, significantly outperforming all baselines. Notably, even\n717\tthe DP-U-Net Small variant outperforms much larger models\n718\tsuch as T-Foley and Mamba-Foley, demonstrating that our DP\n719\tarchitecture can deliver high-fidelity, well-synchronized Foley\n720\taudio with minimal parameters.\n721\tB. Model Complexity and Efficiency Evaluation\n722\tWe assess efficiency using Real-Time Factor (RTF) under\n723\t200 diffusion steps with CFG on 4-second clips, peak GPU\n724\tmemory, and GFLOPs per forward pass. All of the tests\n725\tare conducted on a single NVIDIA V100. As shown in\n726\tTable II, DP-U-Net maintains high generation quality while\n727\tkeeping inference cost moderate, and DP-U-Net Small further reduces computational complexity. In contrast, DP-DiT\n728\texhibits substantially higher RTF and GFLOPs due to its\n729\tdepth. Among baselines, T-Foley achieves the fastest inference speed, Mamba-Foley is the most memory-efficient, and\n730\tDiffWave incurs the highest computational cost. Although TF-\n731\t\n732\t\fDP-U-Net\n733\t\n734\tDP-U-Net Small\n735\t\n736\tTF-DiT\n737\t\n738\tDiffWave\n739\t\n740\tMamba-Foley\n741\t\n742\tT-Foley\n743\t\n744\t0.2\n745\t0.0\n746\t10\n747\t8\n748\t6\n749\t4\n750\t2\n751\t0\n752\t\n753\tFrequency (kHz)\n754\t\n755\tRMS\n756\t\n757\tDP-DiT\n758\t\n759\t0.2\n760\t0.0\n761\t10\n762\t8\n763\t6\n764\t4\n765\t2\n766\t0\n767\t\n768\tFrequency (kHz)\n769\t\n770\tRMS\n771\t\n772\tReference\n773\t\n774\tFig. 5.\n775\t\n776\tReference and generated RMS energy curves and audio examples for the Chime class from the FSD-Kaggle2018 dataset.\n777\t\n778\tDiT requires relatively low computation, its limited generation\n779\tquality restricts its applicability.\n780\t\n781\tR EFERENCES\n782\t[1]\n783\t\n784\tC. Ablation Study and Case Study\n785\tTo evaluate the contribution of each component, we conduct\n786\ta layer-wise ablation study on the trained 5-stage DP-U-Net\n787\tSmall model. Specifically, we independently remove either the\n788\tintra-frame (spectral) attention or the intra-subband (temporal)\n789\tattention at each stage, and measure the resulting performance\n790\tchanges to quantify their individual impact. As shown in\n791\tFigure 4, both modules contribute to the generation quality,\n792\twith the Intra-frame attention providing a larger contribution,\n793\tunderscoring the importance of direct frequency modeling.\n794\tFigure 5 presents a qualitative case study on the Chime\n795\tsound from the FSD-Kaggle2018 dataset. All samples are\n796\tgenerated using the same temporal conditioning derived from\n797\tthe reference audio and the same random seed. While all\n798\tmethods can track the provided RMS conditioning signal,\n799\tsignificant differences emerge in the generated spectrograms.\n800\tSpecifically, only the proposed TF-domain DP models: DPDiT, DP-U-Net, and DP-U-Net Small successfully generate\n801\tmeaningful and complex frequency-domain features that align\n802\twith the reference, highlighting the importance of architectural\n803\tdesign for modeling complex audio spectral patterns.\n804\t\n805\t[2]\n806\t\n807\t[3]\n808\t\n809\t[4]\n810\t\n811\t[5]\n812\t\n813\tVI. C ONCLUSION\n814\tWe propose an efficient waveform diffusion model for Foley\n815\tsound generation based on a fully attention-based U-Net with\n816\tdimension-wise self-attention. By introducing more effective\n817\tinductive biases for temporal–spectral modeling, the proposed\n818\tapproach achieves superior generation quality on the DCASE\n819\tand FSD-Kaggle2018 datasets using only a few million parameters, demonstrating that high-quality Foley sound generation\n820\tcan be achieved with significantly improved efficiency through\n821\twell-designed architectures.\n822\t\n823\t[6]\n824\t\n825\t[7]\n826\t\n827\t[8]\n828\t\n829\tS. Ghose and J. J. Prevost, “Foleygan: Visually guided generative adversarial network-based synchronous sound generation in silent videos,” IEEE\n830\tTransactions on Multimedia, vol. 25, pp. 4508–4519,\n831\t2022.\n832\tZ. Xie, X. Xu, B. Li, M. Wu, and K. Yu, “The x-lance\n833\tsystem for dcase2023 challenge task 7: Foley sound\n834\tsynthesis track b,” Tech. Rep., June, Tech. Rep., 2023.\n835\tA. Qi, X. Xie, and J. Wang, “Mtdiffusion: Multitask diffusion model with dual-unet for foley sound\n836\tgeneration,” in ICASSP 2024-2024 IEEE International\n837\tConference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing\n838\t(ICASSP), IEEE, 2024, pp. 461–465.\n839\tZ. Huang, D. Luo, J. Wang, H. Liao, Z. Li, and\n840\tZ. Wu, “Rhythmic foley: A framework for seamless\n841\taudio-visual alignment in video-to-audio synthesis,” in\n842\tICASSP 2025-2025 IEEE International Conference on\n843\tAcoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP),\n844\tIEEE, 2025, pp. 1–5.\n845\tY. Chung, J. Lee, and J. Nam, “T-foley: A controllable\n846\twaveform-domain diffusion model for temporal-eventguided foley sound synthesis,” in ICASSP 2024-2024\n847\tIEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech\n848\tand Signal Processing (ICASSP), IEEE, 2024, pp. 6820–\n849\t6824.\n850\tY. Yuan et al., “Text-driven foley sound generation with latent diffusion model,” arXiv preprint\n851\tarXiv:2306.10359, 2023.\n852\tH. Liu et al., “Audioldm: Text-to-audio generation with latent diffusion models,” arXiv preprint\n853\tarXiv:2301.12503, 2023.\n854\tH. Liu et al., “Audioldm 2: Learning holistic audio\n855\tgeneration with self-supervised pretraining,” IEEE/ACM\n856\t\n857\t\fTransactions on Audio, Speech, and Language Processing, vol. 32, pp. 2871–2883, 2024.\n858\t[9] P.-J. Ku, A. H. Liu, R. Korostik, S.-F. Huang, S.-W. Fu,\n859\tand A. Jukić, “Generative speech foundation model pretraining for high-quality speech extraction and restoration,” in ICASSP 2025-2025 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing\n860\t(ICASSP), IEEE, 2025, pp. 1–5.\n861\t[10] Y. Luo, Z. Chen, and T. Yoshioka, “Dual-path rnn: Efficient long sequence modeling for time-domain singlechannel speech separation,” in ICASSP 2020-2020 IEEE\n862\tInternational Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP), IEEE, 2020, pp. 46–50.\n863\t[11] Y.-J. Lu, Y. Tsao, and S. Watanabe, “A study on speech\n864\tenhancement based on diffusion probabilistic model,”\n865\tin 2021 Asia-Pacific Signal and Information Processing\n866\tAssociation Annual Summit and Conference (APSIPA\n867\tASC), IEEE, 2021, pp. 659–666.\n868\t[12] R. Shi et al., “Unsupervised single-channel audio separation with diffusion source priors,” in Proceedings of\n869\tthe AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence, vol. 40,\n870\t2026, pp. 25 348–25 356.\n871\t[13] M. F. Colombo, F. Ronchini, L. Comanducci, and F.\n872\tAntonacci, “Mambafoley: Foley sound generation using\n873\tselective state-space models,” in ICASSP 2025-2025\n874\tIEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech\n875\tand Signal Processing (ICASSP), IEEE, 2025, pp. 1–5.\n876\t[14] S. Pascual, G. Bhattacharya, C. Yeh, J. Pons, and J.\n877\tSerrà, “Full-band general audio synthesis with scorebased diffusion,” in ICASSP 2023-2023 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal\n878\tProcessing (ICASSP), IEEE, 2023, pp. 1–5.\n879\t[15] Z. Kong, W. Ping, J. Huang, K. Zhao, and B. Catanzaro, “Diffwave: A versatile diffusion model for audio\n880\tsynthesis,” in International Conference on Learning\n881\tRepresentations.\n882\t[16] A. Van Den Oord et al., “Wavenet: A generative\n883\tmodel for raw audio,” arXiv preprint arXiv:1609.03499,\n884\tvol. 12, p. 1, 2016.\n885\t[17] T. D. Nguyen, J.-H. Kim, Y. Jang, J. Kim, and J. S.\n886\tChung, “Fregrad: Lightweight and fast frequency-aware\n887\tdiffusion vocoder,” in ICASSP 2024-2024 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal\n888\tProcessing (ICASSP), IEEE, 2024, pp. 10 736–10 740.\n889\t[18] A. Iashchenko, P. Andreev, I. Shchekotov, N. Babaev,\n890\tand D. Vetrov, “Undiff: Unsupervised voice restoration\n891\twith unconditional diffusion model,” arXiv preprint\n892\tarXiv:2306.00721, 2023.\n893\t[19] E. Moliner, J. Lehtinen, and V. Välimäki, “Solving audio\n894\tinverse problems with a diffusion model,” in ICASSP\n895\t2023-2023 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics,\n896\tSpeech and Signal Processing (ICASSP), IEEE, 2023,\n897\tpp. 1–5.\n898\t[20] K. Choi et al., “Foley sound synthesis at the dcase 2023\n899\tchallenge,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2304.12521, 2023.\n900\t\n901\t[21]\n902\t\n903\tW. Peebles and S. Xie, “Scalable diffusion models with\n904\ttransformers,” in Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF international conference on computer vision, 2023, pp. 4195–\n905\t4205.\n906\t[22] E. Fonseca et al., “General-purpose tagging of freesound\n907\taudio with audioset labels: Task description, dataset, and\n908\tbaseline,” arXiv preprint arXiv:1807.09902, 2018.\n909\t[23] I. Loshchilov and F. Hutter, “Decoupled weight decay\n910\tregularization,” arXiv preprint arXiv:1711.05101, 2017.\n911\t[24] K. Kilgour, M. Zuluaga, D. Roblek, and M. Sharifi, “Fr\\’echet audio distance: A metric for evaluating music enhancement algorithms,” arXiv preprint\n912\tarXiv:1812.08466, 2018.\n913\t\n914\t\f\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "954b0edb-9335-47f9-9756-0ac54f4d076a", "uuid": "42f187a3-6cee-445d-8b85-8fcaba999873", "timestamp": "2026-08-03T06:51:34.509Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper_2607.29148.txt", "content": "2026 Asia Pacific Signal and Information Processing Association Annual Summit and Conference (APSIPA ASC)\n\nExploring Efficient Waveform Diffusion Models\nfor Foley Sound Generation\nRunwu Shi∗ , Chang Li† , Jiahui Li∗ , Jiang Wang∗ , Yaozhong Kang∗ , Nabeela Khan∗ , Linghan Fang‡ ,\nBenjamin Yen∗ , Takeshi Ashizawa∗ , Kazuhiro Nakadai∗\n∗ Institute of Science Tokyo, Tokyo, Japan\n† University of Science and Technology of China, ‡ Technical University of Munich\n\nE-mail: shirunwu@ra.sc.e.titech.ac.jp\n\narXiv:2607.29148v1 [eess.AS] 31 Jul 2026\n\nAbstract—Recent advances in diffusion models have enabled\nhigh-fidelity Foley sound generation directly in the waveform\nspace. Existing waveform diffusion models primarily rely on timedomain architectures, such as CNN-based U-Nets and DiffWavestyle models, or frequency-domain Transformers modeling temporal dependencies. However, these systems are typically built\nwith large model capacities and substantial computational costs,\nleaving compact and efficient waveform diffusion architectures\nlargely underexplored. In this work, we introduce a DualPath (DP) architecture for waveform diffusion that performs\ndimension-wise self-attention along both subband and frame\naxes in the time–frequency domain. This DP design enables\nfine-grained temporal–spectral modeling while maintaining high\nefficiency. Based on the proposed DP backbone, we develop two\nvariants: DP-DiT and DP-U-Net. Experiments on the DCASE\nand FSD-Kaggle2018 datasets demonstrate their superior performance. Notably, the 3M parameter variant achieves performance\ncomparable to models with more than 50M parameters. Audio\nsamples are available at https://samplesdemo.github.io/DP-Foley/.\n\nI. I NTRODUCTION\n\nFoley sound generation aims to synthesize sound effects that\nare tightly aligned with events and timing, requiring both high\nperceptual fidelity and accurate fine-grained temporal structure.\nA variety of generative paradigms have been explored for this\ntask, including Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs) [1],\nautoencoders [2], and diffusion models [3], [4]. In this work,\nwe focus on raw audio generation and adopt diffusion models\nto generate high-quality Foley sound.\nMany diffusion-based methods for audio generation have\nbeen proposed, targeting diverse output spaces. These approaches can be broadly categorized into three classes: (1)\nwaveform-level generation, which directly synthesizes raw audio signals [5], (2) latent-space generation, which operates in a\ncompressed representation space commonly learned by an autoencoder [6], and (3) acoustic-feature generation, which produces intermediate representations such as mel-spectrograms\nthat are later decoded into waveforms through vocoders [7].\nIn this paper, we adopt waveform-level generation, motivated\nby both fidelity and efficiency considerations. Compared to\nlatent-space or acoustic-feature approaches, waveform-level\ngeneration enables end-to-end modeling without relying on\nintermediate representations and separate decoders, thereby\noffering a more compact formulation. Moreover, with ap-\n\nSound Class Label\nTemporal Condition Signal (RMS)\n\nFig. 1.\n\nFoley Sound\nGeneration\nWaveform\n\nClass and temporal signal guided waveform generation.\n\npropriate architectural design, waveform models can directly\ncapture transient-rich and fine-grained temporal–spectral structures that are crucial for producing convincing Foley sounds.\nAt the same time, it is worth noting that recent large-scale\naudio and music generation systems often operate in compressed domains (e.g., neural audio codec tokens or learned\nlatents) [8], largely because raw waveforms are extremely\nhigh-dimensional and sequences at audio sampling rates are\nprohibitively long to model at scale. Rather than addressing\nthe complexity through model scaling or domain compression,\nin this work, we investigate the limits of direct waveform\ndiffusion under strict parameters and compute budgets. In\nthis context, we study lightweight architecture paradigms that\nenable raw waveform synthesis with only a few million\nparameters while maintaining competitive perceptual quality,\nprioritizing efficiency, deployability, and end-to-end fidelity.\nNevertheless, existing waveform-based diffusion backbones\n(e.g., CNN U-Nets and DiffWave-style architectures) often rely\non very deep networks and relatively large parameter counts\nto model high-dimensional raw signals. Meanwhile, fully selfattention backbones working in the time domain remain costly,\nand recent alternatives that apply temporal attention in the\ntime–frequency (TF) domain to predict complex spectrograms\nto alleviate the long sequence burden [9], yet they typically\nemphasize global temporal modeling and may under-exploit\nfine-grained Intra-frame and Intra-frequency structures that are\ncrucial for expressive Foley synthesis.\nMotivated by the fine-grained frame-path and subband-path\nmodeling paradigm that has proven effective in speech separation and enhancement literature [10], we propose a Dual-Path\n(DP) backbone that performs attention-based modeling along\nboth the Intra-frame and Intra-subband axes in the TF domain.\nBuilding upon this DP module, we instantiate two lightweight\ndiffusion backbones: DP-DiT (DP Diffusion Transformer) and\nDP-U-Net, both of which support class and temporal energy\nsignal conditioning, as shown in Figure 1.\n\n\fWaveform\n\nWaveform\n\nTemporal Attention\n\nxN\n\nCNN Block\n\nC\n\nCNN Block\nResidual\nCNN Block\n\nT\n\nWaveform\n\nWaveform\n\nSTFT + CNN 1D\n\nSTFT + CNN 2D\n\nB×C×F×T\n\nxN\n\nDP Module\nIntra-Subband Attention\n\nResidual\nCNN Block\n\nTransformer\nBlock\n\nLatent Block\n\nB×C×T\n\nTransformer\nBlock\n\nResidual\nCNN Block\n\nxN\n\nCNN Block\nUpSample\n\nT\nWaveform\nSTFT + CNN 2D\n\nCNN Block\nDownSample\n\nIntra-Frame\nPath Attention F\n\nF\n\nT\n\nDownSample\n\n...\n\n...\n\nC\n\nC\nIntra-Subband\nPath Attention\n\nTransformer\nBlock\n\nCNN Block\n\nIntra-Subband Attention\n\n(B×F)×C×T\n\nIntra-Frame Attention\n\n(B×T)×C×F\n\nxN\n\nTransformer\nBlock\n\nResidual\nCNN Block\n\nIntra-Frame Attention\n\nDP Module\nDownSample\n\n...\n\nLatent Block\n\nDP Module\n\nB×C×T\n\nIntra-Subband Attention\n\nUpSample\n\nIntra-Frame Attention\n\nDP Module\nIntra-Subband Attention\nIntra-Frame Attention\n\nUpSample\n\n(a)\n\nWaveform\n\nCNN Block\n\nxN\n\n(b)\n\nWaveform\n\nCNN 1D + iSTFT\n\n(c)\n\nWaveform\n\nCNN 2D + iSTFT\nCNN 2D + iSTFT\n\n(d) Waveform\n\n(e)\n\nxN\n\nWaveform\n\nFig. 2. Five model variants for raw waveform generation: (a) CNN U-Net, (b) DiffWave, (c) TF temporal-attention DiT (TF-DiT), (d) TF Dual Path-attention\nDiT, (e) TF Dual Path-attention U-Net. Conditioning signals (class embedding, diffusion time step embedding, and RMS energy signal) are omitted for clarity.\n\nWe conduct experiments on DCASE and FSD-Kaggle2018\ndatasets, and the results demonstrate that our DP-based models\nconsistently outperform state-of-the-art baselines across all\nobjective and subjective metrics. Notably, our architectures\nachieve superior performance with significantly fewer parameters. This demonstrates that high-quality waveform diffusion\ncan be realized at a remarkably small scale through optimized\ndesign, providing a resource-efficient yet high-fidelity solution\nfor deployable Foley generation.\nII. R ELATED W ORKS\nDiffusion models that directly operate on raw waveforms\nhave been widely explored for various audio tasks, e.g., audio\ngeneration, speech enhancement, and source separation [11],\n[12], [13]. To support direct waveform synthesis, several\ndiffusion-based backbones have been developed in either\nthe time or frequency domain, as summarized in Figure 2.\nWe broadly group them into five categories: CNN U-Net,\nDiffWave-style, TF temporal-attention DiT, TF DP-attention\nDiT, and TF DP-attention U-Net. CNN U-Nets cover most\nFoley waveform generators: T-Foley [5] adopts a CNN UNet and introduces Block-FiLM for temporal control using\nthe RMS energy, while Mamba-Foley [13] augments a similar\nbackbone with a Mamba unit at the bottleneck; both build upon\nthe U-Net design popularized by DAG [14]. DiffWave [15]\nfollows a WaveNet-inspired time-domain architecture [16],\nwhich has been widely applied for vocoder tasks [17]. In\nthe frequency domain, Undiff [18] proposes FFC-AE, a convolutional network operating on complex-valued Short-time\nFourier transform (STFT) spectrograms, and CQT-Diff, a CNN\nU-Net that operates in the CQT domain [19]. There are\nalso transformer-based methods, some of which add the selfattention mechanism into CNN U-Net backbones, and [9] uses\n\na transformer to predict complex STFT coefficients as temporal\nsequences.\nIII. M ETHODOLOGY\nA. Overall Model Architecture\nWe design two backbone paradigms, a DiT-style model and\na U-Net-style model, as illustrated in Figure 2(d,e). Both architectures share the same core module: DP Module equipped\nwith dimension-wise self-attention, including Intra-subband\nand Intra-frame attention, as detailed in the next subsection.\nAccordingly, we name our models DP-DiT and DP-U-Net. DPDiT processes the feature map at the original TF resolution\nwithout any downsampling. In contrast, DP-U-Net follows a\nfive-stage encoder-decoder structure comprising two downsampling stages, a latent bottleneck stage, and two symmetric\nupsampling stages. Both backbones share the same TF frontend and waveform generation head. The input waveform x\nis transformed into a complex STFT spectrogram with size\n2×F ×T , where F and T denote the number of frequency bins\nand time frames, respectively. A 2D convolutional projection\nthen maps this spectrogram to a C-channel feature map of size\nC × F × T for subsequent processing. After being processed\nby the DP-DiT or DP-U-Net, the features are projected back\nand converted to the waveform domain via a transposed convolutional layer and inverse STFT, yielding the predicted noise\nϵ. Specifically, DP-U-Net adopts 2D shuffle-based upsampling\nand downsampling mechanisms. Utilizing PixelShuffle and\nPixelUnshuffle layers combined with convolutions, DP-U-Net\nscales the spatial resolution by a factor of 2 at each stage to\ncapture multi-scale features.\nB. DP block with dimension-wise self-attention\nFigure 3 illustrates the architecture of the DP Module. This\nmodule begins with an optimized Block-FiLM mechanism\n\n\fConv2D\n\nGEGLU\n\nLayerNorm\n\nReshape\n\nReshape\n\nSelf-Attention\n\nTemporal\nCondition\n\nGEGLU\n\nMLP\nLayerNorm\n\nB×C×F×T\n\nEt + Ec\n\nx2\nTransformer Block\n\nRMS signal\n\nIntra-Subband & Intra-Frame\n\nFig. 3. Diagram of the DP module. The transformer block is applied twice\n(Intra-subband and Intra-frame), shown once for clarity. Et and Ec mean\ndiffusion step and class embedding.\n\nfor temporal conditioning [20]. Departing from the original\ncascaded two-layer design, which employs two consecutive\nFiLM-and-convolution blocks. We modify the structure into\na single-layer configuration per module. Despite the reduction\nin depth, we align the operational resolution of Block-FiLM\nwith the intermediate TF representation. This enables finegrained, TF-bin-level control that provides superior temporal\ncontrol accuracy. For class and diffusion step conditioning,\neach module incorporates a diffusion timestep embedding Et\nand a learnable sound class embedding Ec via the AdaLNZero mechanism [21]. This mechanism predicts scaling and\nshifting parameters from the combined embedding Et +Ec for\nmodulation. After this, given the feature map in RB×C×F ×T ,\nwhere B is the batch size, the module captures joint dependencies by alternating between Intra-subband and Intraframe attention. Specifically, the Intra-subband path reshapes\nthe features into R(B×F )×C×T to model temporal dynamics,\nwhile the Intra-frame path reshapes them into R(B×T )×C×F to\ncapture instantaneous spectral relationships. Each stage follows\na Pre-Norm architecture, integrating multi-head self-attention\nand a feed-forward network containing GEGLU and Conv2D\nwithin a residual structure.\nIV. E XPERIMENTS\nA. Dataset\nWe utilized two datasets to evaluate the proposed method.\nFirst is the Detection and Classification of Acoustic Scenes\nand Events (DCASE) task 7 dataset challenge [20]. This\ndataset contains 7 classes of sounds, including dog bark,\nfootstep, gunshot, keyboard, vehicle, rainfall, and coughing.\nThe developing dataset contains 4,850 audio samples, and\nthe evaluation dataset contains 700 audio samples. We use\nthe official development and evaluation datasets for training\nand evaluation. In addition, we utilize the FSD-Kaggle2018\ndataset [22], which comprises around 11,000 clips across 41\nsound event categories. We use the provided training set with\n9,470 clips for training and randomly choose 10 samples per\nclass for testing. All audio samples are sampled at 22,050 Hz\nwith a duration of 4 seconds.\nB. Implementation Details\nFor all DP-DiT and DP-U-Net variants, we compute the\nSTFT using a window size of 510 and a hop length of 255.\nThis yields a complex-valued TF representation of shape 352 ×\n256 (time × frequency bins) for each 4-second audio clip. The\n\ntemporal conditional RMS signal used for temporal control is\nextracted from the input waveform with a frame length of 512\nand a hop length of 128. For DP-DiT, the channel dimension is\nset to C = 64. The DP module is repeated 32 times, resulting\nin a total of 3.27M parameters. For DP-U-Net, we consider\ntwo model variants that share the same channel dimension\nC = 64 but differ in the number of block repetitions. The\nstandard DP-U-Net adopts a five-stage encoder–decoder layout\nwith DP module repetitions of {1, 2, 2, 2, 1}, yielding 8.57M\nparameters. We further introduce a lightweight variant, DP-UNet Small, which uses the same five-stage layout with a single\nDP module at each stage, resulting in only 3.26M parameters.\nWe train all of the models for 500 epochs using the AdamW\noptimizer [23] with a learning rate of 1 × 10−4 . Denoising\ntraining is conducted for 200 steps with a linear noise schedule\nranging from 10−4 to 2 × 10−2 . For sampling, we adopt the\nDDPM scheduler with classifier-free guidance (scale = 1.2),\nwhere 10% of the conditions are randomly dropped during\ntraining for unconditional generation.\nC. Compared Baselines\nWe compare the proposed DP-DiT and DP-U-Net with several representative baselines, including T-Foley [5], MambaFoley [13], DiffWave [15], and a TF-domain DiT model. TFoley is a CNN-based U-Net with a recurrent unit in the\nlatent stage for temporal modeling, for which we use the\nofficial pretrained checkpoint and original sampling settings1 .\nFor Mamba-Foley, we implement it using the official codebase\nand train it from scratch under the same training settings as our\nmethods2 . Both models are substantially larger than ours, with\n74.09M and 58.81M parameters, respectively. For the sampling\nsetting, we use their original guidance scale. We also include\nDiffWave [15], augmented with Block-FiLM conditioning for\ntemporal control, configured with 24 residual blocks and a\nchannel dimension of 98, resulting in 12.58M parameters.\nIn addition, we compare with a TF-domain DiT baseline\nthat adopts the same Transformer block as the proposed\nmethod, and predicts concatenated real and imaginary spectrogram components along the temporal dimension, as shown\nin Figure 2 (c). The depth is set to 16, resulting in 21.39M\nparameters. Directly applying DiT in the time domain would be\ncomputationally expensive due to the high temporal resolution\nof feature maps produced by the 1D convolutional head, which\nrequires specifically designed downsampling mechanisms that\nare beyond the scope of this work, and thus a time-domain\nDiT baseline is not considered. For all methods, we use 200\nsampling steps and adopt the same DDPM sampling strategy.\nD. Evaluation Metrics\nThe evaluation includes both objective and subjective measures. For the objective evaluation, we use the Fréchet Audio\nDistance (FAD) [24] to assess perceptual quality and the\nEvent-L1 Distance (E-L1) to evaluate the temporal accuracy of\n1 https://github.com/YoonjinXD/T-foley\n2 https://github.com/FurioColombo/mamba-foley\n\n\fTABLE I\nO BJECTIVE AND SUBJECTIVE EVALUATION RESULTS . T HE BEST RESULTS ARE SHOWN IN BOLD . D URING TESTING , THE RMS SIGNAL IS EXTRACTED FROM\nTHE EVALUATION DATASET. R EAL DATA’ S FAD IS CALCULATED BETWEEN THE TRAINING AND EVALUATION DATASETS .\n\nDCASE (7 classes)\n\nFSD-Kaggle2018 (41 classes)\n\nSubjective evaluation\n\nMethod\n\nParams\n\nE-L1 ↓\n\nFAD-P ↓\n\nFAD-V ↓\n\nE-L1 ↓\n\nFAD-P ↓\n\nFAD-V ↓\n\nQuality ↑\n\nTime Align ↑\n\nReal Data\n\n–\n\n–\n\n18.82\n\n3.95\n\n–\n\n69.71\n\n8.41\n\n–\n\n–\n\nT-Foley [5]\nMamba-Foley [13]\nDiffwave [15]\nTF-DiT\n\n74.09M\n58.81M\n12.58M\n21.39M\n\n0.035\n0.021\n0.019\n0.170\n\n34.97\n29.65\n52.00\n116.76\n\n10.11\n6.03\n11.06\n23.87\n\n0.058\n0.028\n0.019\n0.110\n\n87.32\n72.99\n100.26\n130.66\n\n17.14\n12.22\n15.83\n32.83\n\n2.53\n3.25\n2.45\n0.79\n\n3.04\n3.66\n2.84\n0.82\n\nDP-DiT\nDP-U-Net\nDP-U-Net Small\n\n3.27M\n8.57M\n3.26M\n\n0.009\n0.009\n0.009\n\n30.07\n27.48\n27.98\n\n7.02\n5.34\n6.34\n\n0.007\n0.006\n0.007\n\n67.09\n62.77\n75.13\n\n13.53\n11.55\n11.98\n\n3.97\n4.07\n3.79\n\n4.46\n4.47\n4.30\n\nTABLE II\nI NFERENCE EFFICIENCY AND MODEL COMPLEXITY.\nMethod\n\nRTF ↓\n\nGFLOPs ↓\n\nMemory (GB)\n\nT-Foley [5]\nMamba-Foley [13]\nDiffWave [15]\nTF-DiT\n\n2.61\n4.05\n6.24\n6.85\n\n54.15\n64.67\n1302.84\n9.58\n\n2.11\n0.44\n9.74\n0.12\n\nDP-DiT\nDP-U-Net\nDP-U-Net Small\n\n31.06\n4.37\n3.20\n\n349.53\n196.78\n78.38\n\n1.12\n1.22\n1.15\n\n140\n\nA. Objective and Subjective Evaluation\nThe left panel of Table I presents the objective results for\nDCASE and FSD-Kaggle2018. Overall, the proposed DP-DiT\nand DP-U-Net variants achieve strong performance across all\nmetrics with substantially fewer parameters than baselines.\nSpecifically, DP-U-Net obtains the lowest E-L1 and FAD\nscores, indicating superior temporal alignment and perceptual\nquality, while DP-U-Net Small (3.26M) remains highly competitive. The performance gap between DP-U-Net and DP-DiT\nfurther underscores the architectural advantage of U-Net over\nDiT for waveform generation. Among the baselines, MambaFoley outperforms T-Foley, suggesting the importance of latent\nspace design. Diffwave achieves relatively low E-L1 but suffers\nfrom high FAD scores, indicating limited perceptual fidelity.\nConversely, TF-DiT performs poorly, confirming that direct\nspectrogram modeling along the temporal axis is inadequate.\nIn summary, our models benefit from more effective inductive\nbiases, enabling substantial parameter reduction while maintaining strong temporal accuracy and perceptual quality.\nSubjective evaluation results are summarized in the right\npanel of Table I. Our proposed DP-U-Net consistently achieves\nthe highest scores in both Audio Quality and Temporal Align-\n\nw/o Intra-frame\nw/o Intra-subband\n\n60\n50\n\nFAD-V\n\n120\n100\n\n40\n30\n20\n\n80\n\nV. R ESULTS\n\n70\n\nw/o Intra-frame\nw/o Intra-subband\n\n160\n\nFAD-P\n\ngenerated sound, following previous works [5], [13]. The FAD\nis computed using VGGish (FAD-V) and PANNs-CNN14-32k\n(FAD-P) embeddings implemented in the fadtk toolkit. The\nE-L1 distance is computed on RMS energy feature\nPNof audio\nwaveform, and is defined as E-L1(E, Ê) = N1 i=1 ∥Ei −\nÊi ∥1 , where Ei and Êi denote the RMS energy features of\nthe i-th frame from the reference and generated audio [20].\nFor subjective evaluation, we conduct a 20-minute listening\ntest with nine participants using a 5-point Likert scale. Two\nkey dimensions are evaluated: Audio Quality, which measures\nperceptual naturalness, and Time Alignment, which measures\nsynchronization with the conditioning signal. All test samples are provided on the demo page. For each sample, we\npresent the reference audio together with the waveform and\nRMS energy curves, allowing participants to assess temporal\nsynchronization through both listening and visual inspection.\n\n1\n\nBaseline (Full): 75.13\n2\n\n3\n\nU-Net stage\n\n4\n\n10\n5\n\n1\n\nBaseline (Full): 11.98\n2\n\n3\n\nU-Net stage\n\n4\n\n5\n\nFig. 4. Layer-wise ablation on the 5-stage DP-U-Net Small by independently\nomitting a single attention block at each stage.\n\nment, significantly outperforming all baselines. Notably, even\nthe DP-U-Net Small variant outperforms much larger models\nsuch as T-Foley and Mamba-Foley, demonstrating that our DP\narchitecture can deliver high-fidelity, well-synchronized Foley\naudio with minimal parameters.\nB. Model Complexity and Efficiency Evaluation\nWe assess efficiency using Real-Time Factor (RTF) under\n200 diffusion steps with CFG on 4-second clips, peak GPU\nmemory, and GFLOPs per forward pass. All of the tests\nare conducted on a single NVIDIA V100. As shown in\nTable II, DP-U-Net maintains high generation quality while\nkeeping inference cost moderate, and DP-U-Net Small further reduces computational complexity. In contrast, DP-DiT\nexhibits substantially higher RTF and GFLOPs due to its\ndepth. Among baselines, T-Foley achieves the fastest inference speed, Mamba-Foley is the most memory-efficient, and\nDiffWave incurs the highest computational cost. Although TF-\n\n\fDP-U-Net\n\nDP-U-Net Small\n\nTF-DiT\n\nDiffWave\n\nMamba-Foley\n\nT-Foley\n\n0.2\n0.0\n10\n8\n6\n4\n2\n0\n\nFrequency (kHz)\n\nRMS\n\nDP-DiT\n\n0.2\n0.0\n10\n8\n6\n4\n2\n0\n\nFrequency (kHz)\n\nRMS\n\nReference\n\nFig. 5.\n\nReference and generated RMS energy curves and audio examples for the Chime class from the FSD-Kaggle2018 dataset.\n\nDiT requires relatively low computation, its limited generation\nquality restricts its applicability.\n\nR EFERENCES\n[1]\n\nC. Ablation Study and Case Study\nTo evaluate the contribution of each component, we conduct\na layer-wise ablation study on the trained 5-stage DP-U-Net\nSmall model. Specifically, we independently remove either the\nintra-frame (spectral) attention or the intra-subband (temporal)\nattention at each stage, and measure the resulting performance\nchanges to quantify their individual impact. As shown in\nFigure 4, both modules contribute to the generation quality,\nwith the Intra-frame attention providing a larger contribution,\nunderscoring the importance of direct frequency modeling.\nFigure 5 presents a qualitative case study on the Chime\nsound from the FSD-Kaggle2018 dataset. All samples are\ngenerated using the same temporal conditioning derived from\nthe reference audio and the same random seed. While all\nmethods can track the provided RMS conditioning signal,\nsignificant differences emerge in the generated spectrograms.\nSpecifically, only the proposed TF-domain DP models: DPDiT, DP-U-Net, and DP-U-Net Small successfully generate\nmeaningful and complex frequency-domain features that align\nwith the reference, highlighting the importance of architectural\ndesign for modeling complex audio spectral patterns.\n\n[2]\n\n[3]\n\n[4]\n\n[5]\n\nVI. C ONCLUSION\nWe propose an efficient waveform diffusion model for Foley\nsound generation based on a fully attention-based U-Net with\ndimension-wise self-attention. By introducing more effective\ninductive biases for temporal–spectral modeling, the proposed\napproach achieves superior generation quality on the DCASE\nand FSD-Kaggle2018 datasets using only a few million parameters, demonstrating that high-quality Foley sound generation\ncan be achieved with significantly improved efficiency through\nwell-designed architectures.\n\n[6]\n\n[7]\n\n[8]\n\nS. Ghose and J. J. Prevost, “Foleygan: Visually guided generative adversarial network-based synchronous sound generation in silent videos,” IEEE\nTransactions on Multimedia, vol. 25, pp. 4508–4519,\n2022.\nZ. Xie, X. Xu, B. Li, M. Wu, and K. Yu, “The x-lance\nsystem for dcase2023 challenge task 7: Foley sound\nsynthesis track b,” Tech. Rep., June, Tech. Rep., 2023.\nA. Qi, X. Xie, and J. Wang, “Mtdiffusion: Multitask diffusion model with dual-unet for foley sound\ngeneration,” in ICASSP 2024-2024 IEEE International\nConference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing\n(ICASSP), IEEE, 2024, pp. 461–465.\nZ. Huang, D. Luo, J. Wang, H. Liao, Z. Li, and\nZ. Wu, “Rhythmic foley: A framework for seamless\naudio-visual alignment in video-to-audio synthesis,” in\nICASSP 2025-2025 IEEE International Conference on\nAcoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP),\nIEEE, 2025, pp. 1–5.\nY. Chung, J. Lee, and J. Nam, “T-foley: A controllable\nwaveform-domain diffusion model for temporal-eventguided foley sound synthesis,” in ICASSP 2024-2024\nIEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech\nand Signal Processing (ICASSP), IEEE, 2024, pp. 6820–\n6824.\nY. Yuan et al., “Text-driven foley sound generation with latent diffusion model,” arXiv preprint\narXiv:2306.10359, 2023.\nH. Liu et al., “Audioldm: Text-to-audio generation with latent diffusion models,” arXiv preprint\narXiv:2301.12503, 2023.\nH. Liu et al., “Audioldm 2: Learning holistic audio\ngeneration with self-supervised pretraining,” IEEE/ACM\n\n\fTransactions on Audio, Speech, and Language Processing, vol. 32, pp. 2871–2883, 2024.\n[9] P.-J. Ku, A. H. Liu, R. Korostik, S.-F. Huang, S.-W. Fu,\nand A. Jukić, “Generative speech foundation model pretraining for high-quality speech extraction and restoration,” in ICASSP 2025-2025 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing\n(ICASSP), IEEE, 2025, pp. 1–5.\n[10] Y. Luo, Z. Chen, and T. Yoshioka, “Dual-path rnn: Efficient long sequence modeling for time-domain singlechannel speech separation,” in ICASSP 2020-2020 IEEE\nInternational Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP), IEEE, 2020, pp. 46–50.\n[11] Y.-J. Lu, Y. Tsao, and S. Watanabe, “A study on speech\nenhancement based on diffusion probabilistic model,”\nin 2021 Asia-Pacific Signal and Information Processing\nAssociation Annual Summit and Conference (APSIPA\nASC), IEEE, 2021, pp. 659–666.\n[12] R. Shi et al., “Unsupervised single-channel audio separation with diffusion source priors,” in Proceedings of\nthe AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence, vol. 40,\n2026, pp. 25 348–25 356.\n[13] M. F. Colombo, F. Ronchini, L. Comanducci, and F.\nAntonacci, “Mambafoley: Foley sound generation using\nselective state-space models,” in ICASSP 2025-2025\nIEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech\nand Signal Processing (ICASSP), IEEE, 2025, pp. 1–5.\n[14] S. Pascual, G. Bhattacharya, C. Yeh, J. Pons, and J.\nSerrà, “Full-band general audio synthesis with scorebased diffusion,” in ICASSP 2023-2023 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal\nProcessing (ICASSP), IEEE, 2023, pp. 1–5.\n[15] Z. Kong, W. Ping, J. Huang, K. Zhao, and B. Catanzaro, “Diffwave: A versatile diffusion model for audio\nsynthesis,” in International Conference on Learning\nRepresentations.\n[16] A. Van Den Oord et al., “Wavenet: A generative\nmodel for raw audio,” arXiv preprint arXiv:1609.03499,\nvol. 12, p. 1, 2016.\n[17] T. D. Nguyen, J.-H. Kim, Y. Jang, J. Kim, and J. S.\nChung, “Fregrad: Lightweight and fast frequency-aware\ndiffusion vocoder,” in ICASSP 2024-2024 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal\nProcessing (ICASSP), IEEE, 2024, pp. 10 736–10 740.\n[18] A. Iashchenko, P. Andreev, I. Shchekotov, N. Babaev,\nand D. Vetrov, “Undiff: Unsupervised voice restoration\nwith unconditional diffusion model,” arXiv preprint\narXiv:2306.00721, 2023.\n[19] E. Moliner, J. Lehtinen, and V. Välimäki, “Solving audio\ninverse problems with a diffusion model,” in ICASSP\n2023-2023 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics,\nSpeech and Signal Processing (ICASSP), IEEE, 2023,\npp. 1–5.\n[20] K. Choi et al., “Foley sound synthesis at the dcase 2023\nchallenge,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2304.12521, 2023.\n\n[21]\n\nW. Peebles and S. Xie, “Scalable diffusion models with\ntransformers,” in Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF i